  "narration": {
    "text": "In Vu Lang commune, among the limestone hills of Bac Son in Lang Son province, The Hung Organic Cooperative grows kudzu on its own home land. Clouds still drift over the peaks while the vines cover every trellis. Purple kudzu flowers bloom across the field, and the roots quietly grow underground. At harvest time, whole roots are dug up, big and firm, still covered in soil. They are weighed right at the workshop, washed thoroughly, and ground to press out the starch milk. The starch milk is stirred and left to settle at the bottom of the tank. Then it is rinsed, stirred, settled and filtered again, several times, until the starch is pure and white. The wet starch is shaped by hand, laid out on trays, and dried in a multi-tier kiln. The temperature is set and controlled, so it dries evenly, whatever the weather. No chemicals, no bleaching. The white color is the starch's own. Straight out of the kiln, it is bagged, and available all year round. Alongside the kudzu, we grow Canh mandarins to the VietGAP standard, left to ripen naturally on the tree. Around November of the lunar calendar, they are hand-picked in season, sorted, packed with care, and shipped across Vietnam. The Hung Organic. Real produce, from the hills of Bac Son.",
